    "body": "<!-- pr-reviewer: e427e2bf362a002401640007925adba23f987b5a -->\n**Independent review (v0.14.0 cut), static.** I read the diff, `contour_comp.rs::push_wear`, the `fanuc.cps` comp hooks and the golden. Verdict: **clean, no blocking items.**\n\n### Checked\n- The wear span's `programmed` is exactly the centre moves `first..=last`: lead-in, ring, lead-out. The sim and verify read the same moves, so the proved path is what posts.\n- Golden (10x10 box, D6): `G01 G42 X13 Y5 ... D01` on the 6 mm straight lead-in, which is collinear with the next block, so start-up is clean. The ring is the r=3 centre ring with R3 G03 corners. `G01 G40 X13 Y11` comes on the collinear lead-out. The lead ends (13,-1) and (13,11) stand 3.16 from the box corners, so there is no gouge at zero delta. This repeats per depth (Z5 / 2.5 / 0), with each pass engaging and cancelling.\n- Side: the part is left of travel and the tool right, so G42 is geometrically correct. A positive delta moves away from the material. A full radius left in D01 by mistake undercuts (leaves stock, the safe direction) rather than gouging.\n- Comment `(WEAR COMP - D01 HOLDS THE WEAR DELTA ONLY, NOT THE TOOL RADIUS)`: once per section, `wearNoted` resets in `endPreamble`, and the D number matches the engage block's `compD`.\n- Every Control door refusal is kept and named \"wear compensation ...\". Skipping the full-radius room check is correct for a centre path. An inside centre arc smaller than |delta| makes Fanuc raise its interference alarm (a refusal on the control, not a gouge).\n- Haas / Mazak / Sinumerik / Heidenhain / lathe refuse by name and the gate blocks (`the_other_posts_refuse_wear_by_name_and_the_gate_blocks`). `wear_comp` and the span's `wear` flag are written only when on, so older files and goldens are unchanged.\n- The golden matches the PR body's claim.\n\n### Pre-existing, not this PR (worth its own issue)\n`side_of` and `contour.rs` call \"climb\" the travel that keeps the material **left** of the cutter. Here that is +Y along the right side of the box, CCW around an outside contour. With M3 (a CW spindle) the cutter then rolls against the feed, which is conventional (up) milling. Climb keeps the material on the right, the textbook \"G41 = climb\". The G41/G42 word still matches the geometry, so nothing gouges here, but the Climb/Conventional parameter may be the wrong way round on main. Please verify it against a handbook.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
